1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,189
Videos 26 a and 26 b have everything for the E C E 358 midterm: Chapters 1, 2 and 3 of the lecture notes, without TCP congestion control. This is part 1.

2
00:00:15,639 --> 00:00:26,306
Each slide has a picture, the rule, the formula, and one example, in lecture order. Part 2 ends with the past midterm questions and a formula sheet.

3
00:00:26,756 --> 00:00:35,737
The midterm covers Chapters 1 to 3, without TCP congestion control. This scope comes from the instructor's announcement.

4
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:44,059
Chapter 1, the introduction: edge and core, switching, delays, throughput, and layers. Problem Set 1 practices it.

5
00:00:44,509 --> 00:00:51,336
Chapter 2, the application layer: HTTP and DNS. Problem Set 2 practices it.

6
00:00:51,786 --> 00:01:03,093
Chapter 3, the transport layer: UDP, reliable transfer, Go-Back-N, Selective Repeat, and TCP. Congestion control is not on this midterm.

7
00:01:03,543 --> 00:01:12,716
The exam has 5 multi-part questions. Question 1 has 20 multiple choice parts, worth 0 point 5 marks each.

8
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:16,878
Question 2 has 10 parts, all from the question bank.

9
00:01:17,328 --> 00:01:21,723
Question 3 has many calculations, mainly from Chapter 1.

10
00:01:22,173 --> 00:01:27,463
Question 4 is about ARQ protocols: concepts and calculations.

11
00:01:27,913 --> 00:01:32,564
Question 5 is about TCP, without congestion control.

12
00:01:33,014 --> 00:01:45,707
Not examined: probability, TCP congestion control, and header layouts. After the three chapters, part 2 shows the past midterm question types and a formula sheet.

13
00:01:46,157 --> 00:01:53,880
Hosts, also called end systems, run the applications. Clients and servers are hosts at the network edge.

14
00:01:54,330 --> 00:02:05,338
An access network connects a host to its first router, the edge router. Examples: home WiFi, institutional Ethernet, and 4G or 5G.

15
00:02:05,788 --> 00:02:10,609
The network core is a mesh of routers. It connects the access networks.

16
00:02:11,059 --> 00:02:18,206
A protocol defines the format and the order of the messages, and the actions on transmission and receipt.

17
00:02:18,656 --> 00:02:25,867
A host breaks a message into packets of L bits. It sends each packet into the link one bit at a time.

18
00:02:26,317 --> 00:02:34,999
The transmission delay is the time to push all L bits into the link: d trans equals L over R. R is the link rate.

19
00:02:35,449 --> 00:02:43,513
Slide example: L is 1500 bytes, times 8, which is 12 000 bits.

20
00:02:43,963 --> 00:02:50,705
R is 100 Mbps, which is 100 times 10 to the 6 bits per second.

21
00:02:51,155 --> 00:03:01,565
L over R is 1 point 2 times 10 to the minus 4 seconds, or 0 point 1 2 ms. That is when the last bit leaves.

22
00:03:02,015 --> 00:03:10,357
Units: 1 byte is 8 bits. For rates, kilo, mega and giga are 10 to the 3, 10 to the 6 and 10 to the 9.

23
00:03:10,807 --> 00:03:18,807
A physical link lies between a transmitter and a receiver. In guided media, the signal moves in a solid medium.

24
00:03:19,257 --> 00:03:31,033
Twisted pair is two copper wires. Category 5 carries 100 Mbps and 1 Gbps; Category 6 carries 10 Gbps.

25
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:36,496
Coaxial cable has two concentric conductors and carries many channels.

26
00:03:36,946 --> 00:03:43,261
Fiber carries light pulses, one pulse per bit, at tens to hundreds of Gbps.

27
00:03:43,711 --> 00:03:54,463
In unguided media, the signal moves freely as radio: WiFi, 4G cellular, and satellite with 270 ms of delay.

28
00:03:54,913 --> 00:04:02,465
The bit error rate, BER, is the number of flipped bits at the receiver, divided by the number of bits sent.

29
00:04:02,915 --> 00:04:11,341
Typical BER: wireless 10 to the minus 7, copper 10 to the minus 10, fiber 10 to the minus 12.

30
00:04:11,791 --> 00:04:18,703
Example: 10 to the 12 bits give 1 error over fiber, and 10 to the 5 errors over wireless.

31
00:04:19,153 --> 00:04:29,948
Compare the powers: 10 to the minus 7 is 10 to the 5 times 10 to the minus 12. So wireless has the most bit errors, and fiber the fewest.

32
00:04:30,398 --> 00:04:34,921
Each router in the network core does two jobs: forwarding and routing.

33
00:04:35,371 --> 00:04:42,112
Forwarding is a local action. The router looks up the header value of a packet in its forwarding table.

34
00:04:42,562 --> 00:04:51,586
A packet with header 0 1 1 1 arrives. The table maps 0 1 1 1 to link 2, so the packet leaves on link 2.

35
00:04:52,036 --> 00:04:59,012
Routing is a global action. A routing algorithm finds the paths and creates the forwarding tables.

36
00:04:59,462 --> 00:05:08,507
Store and forward: a router must receive the whole packet before it sends it on. In slot 1, packet 1 crosses link 1.

37
00:05:08,957 --> 00:05:20,243
One slot is L over R. With L equal to 10 kilobits and R equal to 100 Mbps, L over R is 0 point 1 ms.

38
00:05:20,693 --> 00:05:28,309
In slot 2, packet 1 crosses link 2. So one packet over N links takes N times L over R.

39
00:05:28,759 --> 00:05:34,391
Packets 2 and 3 follow, one slot apart. Both links send at the same time.

40
00:05:34,841 --> 00:05:42,649
The last packet arrives at the end of slot 4. P packets over N links take N plus P minus 1 slots.

41
00:05:43,099 --> 00:05:49,712
Here that is 4 slots, times 0 point 1 ms: 0 point 4 ms.

42
00:05:50,162 --> 00:05:59,101
A and B send at 100 Mb/s each, but the output link to E has only 1 point 5 Mb/s.

43
00:05:59,551 --> 00:06:05,609
Packets arrive faster than the link sends them. They wait in the queue, also called the buffer.

44
00:06:06,059 --> 00:06:11,542
The buffer is finite. A packet that arrives at a full buffer is dropped, or lost.

45
00:06:11,992 --> 00:06:18,883
The previous node or the source may send it again, or nobody does. That depends on the protocol.

46
00:06:19,333 --> 00:06:26,458
When the arrival rate stays above R, the queue grows until the buffer is full, and then packets are lost.

47
00:06:26,908 --> 00:06:35,143
Circuit switching reserves resources for each call. Here each link has 4 circuits, and the call uses one on each link.

48
00:06:35,593 --> 00:06:42,419
The circuit is not shared. Performance is guaranteed, but the circuit is idle when the call is silent.

49
00:06:42,869 --> 00:06:50,379
FDM, frequency division multiplexing, gives each call its own frequency band, all the time.

50
00:06:50,829 --> 00:06:59,362
TDM, time division multiplexing, gives each call periodic time slots, with the whole band in its slots.

51
00:06:59,812 --> 00:07:08,068
In TDM, a call sends at the full band rate in its own slots. With 4 calls, that is 1 slot in 4.

52
00:07:08,518 --> 00:07:19,419
Slide 28: a 1 Gb/s link. Each user sends at 100 Mb/s when active, and is active 10 percent of the time.

53
00:07:19,869 --> 00:07:30,643
Circuit switching reserves 100 Mb/s per user: 1 Gb/s divided by 100 Mb/s is 10 users.

54
00:07:31,093 --> 00:07:38,282
Packet switching shares the link among 35 users. Each user is idle 90 percent of the time.

55
00:07:38,732 --> 00:07:51,020
At one moment, for example, 4 users are active. 4 times 100 Mb/s is 400 Mb/s, which fits in 1 Gb/s.

56
00:07:51,470 --> 00:08:04,334
There is a problem only when more than 10 users are active at once, and the slide shows that this is rare. So packet switching serves 35 users, and circuit switching only 10.

57
00:08:04,784 --> 00:08:09,840
The probability calculation behind this is not examined on this midterm.

58
00:08:10,290 --> 00:08:17,821
Slide 29 gives the limit: with too much traffic, the buffers overflow, and packets are delayed and lost.

59
00:08:18,271 --> 00:08:23,839
The Internet is a network of networks. Hosts connect through access ISPs.

60
00:08:24,289 --> 00:08:34,443
Access ISPs connect to regional ISPs, and those connect to tier-1 ISPs with national and international coverage.

61
00:08:34,893 --> 00:08:40,952
At an IXP, an Internet exchange point, many ISPs connect to each other.

62
00:08:41,402 --> 00:08:49,914
Content providers such as Google run private networks that connect their data centers, often around the tier-1 ISPs.

63
00:08:50,364 --> 00:08:58,321
At each node, a packet meets four delays. d nodal is d proc plus d queue plus d trans plus d prop.

64
00:08:58,771 --> 00:09:02,995
d proc: the router checks bit errors and chooses the output link.

65
00:09:03,445 --> 00:09:08,096
d queue: the wait in the output buffer. It depends on the congestion.

66
00:09:08,546 --> 00:09:13,431
d trans is L over R: the time to push all bits onto the link.

67
00:09:13,881 --> 00:09:22,607
d prop is d over s: the time for one bit to cross the link. s is about 2 times 10 to the 8 meters per second.

68
00:09:23,057 --> 00:09:35,131
Example: 12 000 bits at 10 Mbps. d trans is 12 000 over 10 to the 7: 1 point 2 ms.

69
00:09:35,581 --> 00:09:45,544
A 500 km link: d prop is 5 times 10 to the 5, over 2 times 10 to the 8: 2 point 5 ms.

70
00:09:45,994 --> 00:09:53,503
d trans depends only on L and R. d prop depends only on the distance d and the speed s.

71
00:09:53,953 --> 00:10:02,913
a is the packet arrival rate. L times a bits arrive per second, and R bits leave. Traffic intensity is L a over R.

72
00:10:03,363 --> 00:10:08,185
Near 0, the average queueing delay is small. Near 1, it is large.

73
00:10:08,635 --> 00:10:13,712
Above 1, more work arrives than the link can serve. The delay is infinite.

74
00:10:14,162 --> 00:10:25,319
Example: L a is 1000 times 800, which is 800 000 bits per second. Over 10 to the 6, that is 0 point 8.

75
00:10:25,769 --> 00:10:37,055
Problem Set 1, Problem 5: 4 packets arrive at once. Packet 1 waits 0, packet 2 waits L over R, packet 4 waits 3 L over R.

76
00:10:37,505 --> 00:10:46,806
The average is 6 over 4, which is 1 point 5 L over R. In general: N minus 1, times L, over 2 R.

77
00:10:47,256 --> 00:10:53,357
Traffic intensity compares bits per second with bits per second: L times a, with R.

78
00:10:53,807 --> 00:10:58,074
Throughput is the rate at which the receiver gets bits from the sender.

79
00:10:58,524 --> 00:11:05,799
With links in a row, the slowest link is the bottleneck. The throughput is the min of R s and R c.

80
00:11:06,249 --> 00:11:15,017
Example: the min of 2 and 1 Mbps is 1 Mbps. An 8 megabit file takes 8 seconds.

81
00:11:15,467 --> 00:11:25,557
Slide 51: 10 connections share a backbone link of rate R. Each gets R over 10, here 0 point 5 Mbps.

82
00:11:26,007 --> 00:11:36,247
So the throughput is the min of R s, R c and R over 10: 0 point 5 Mbps. The file takes 16 seconds.

83
00:11:36,697 --> 00:11:44,057
The throughput is the smallest rate on the path. In practice, R c or R s is often the bottleneck.

84
00:11:44,507 --> 00:11:51,121
Problem Set 1, Problem 6: R times d prop is the number of bits in the link at one time.

85
00:11:51,571 --> 00:12:00,957
1000 km is 10 to the 6 meters. d prop is 10 to the 6 over 2 times 10 to the 8: 5 ms.

86
00:12:01,407 --> 00:12:08,597
R times d prop is 10 to the 6 times 5 times 10 to the minus 3: 5000 bits.

87
00:12:09,047 --> 00:12:15,916
One bit is 10 to the 6 meters over 5000: 200 meters, which is s over R.

88
00:12:16,366 --> 00:12:22,403
For a file smaller than R times d prop, the most bits in the link is the file size.

89
00:12:22,853 --> 00:12:30,256
Each layer gives a service to the layer above, and uses the layer below. The Internet stack has five layers.

90
00:12:30,706 --> 00:12:38,023
Application: network applications, such as HTTP, IMAP, SMTP and DNS.

91
00:12:38,473 --> 00:12:44,041
Transport: process to process data transfer, with TCP or UDP.

92
00:12:44,491 --> 00:12:50,721
Network: routes datagrams from source to destination, with IP and routing protocols.

93
00:12:51,171 --> 00:12:56,205
Link: transfer between neighbors, such as Ethernet, WiFi and PPP.

94
00:12:56,655 --> 00:12:58,725
Physical: the bits on the wire.

95
00:12:59,175 --> 00:13:06,407
Layers make the system modular. A change in the physical layer needs no change in the transport layer.

96
00:13:06,857 --> 00:13:16,094
Devices differ. A host has all five layers. A router has network, link and physical. A link layer switch has link and physical.

97
00:13:16,544 --> 00:13:21,515
Encapsulation. At the source, the application creates a message, M.

98
00:13:21,965 --> 00:13:25,591
Transport adds H t. The unit is a segment.

99
00:13:26,041 --> 00:13:30,905
Network adds H n. The unit is a datagram, or IP packet.

100
00:13:31,355 --> 00:13:36,881
Link adds H l. The unit is a frame, and the physical layer sends its bits.

101
00:13:37,331 --> 00:13:42,259
At the destination, each layer removes its own header, in reverse order.

102
00:13:42,709 --> 00:13:53,631
Headers are overhead: Ethernet 14, IP 20 and TCP 20 bytes. A 100 byte message gives a 154 byte frame.

103
00:13:54,081 --> 00:14:04,023
That is 54 over 154, about 35 percent. A 1000 byte message gives only 5 point 1 percent.

104
00:14:04,473 --> 00:14:10,361
A frame holds all the headers, H l, H n and H t, and then the message M.

105
00:14:10,811 --> 00:14:19,344
Chapter 2: the application layer. Two protocols for the midterm: HTTP for the web, and DNS for names.

106
00:14:19,794 --> 00:14:24,957
First, the principles: app architectures, and what an app needs from transport.

107
00:14:25,407 --> 00:14:30,633
Client-server: the server is always on and has a permanent IP address.

108
00:14:31,083 --> 00:14:35,670
Clients connect from time to time. Their IP addresses can change.

109
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:42,115
Clients do not talk directly to each other. HTTP and FTP work this way.

110
00:14:42,565 --> 00:14:49,221
Peer-to-peer: no always-on server. Peers ask other peers for service, and serve them in return.

111
00:14:49,671 --> 00:15:00,167
Our own example: 4 peers, a file of 100 megabytes. Each peer gets 100 over 4, so 25 megabytes, from the source.

112
00:15:00,617 --> 00:15:10,643
It gets the other 3 pieces, 3 times 25, so 75 megabytes, from the peers. New peers add capacity: self-scalability.

113
00:15:11,093 --> 00:15:17,664
A process is a program running in a host. Processes in different hosts exchange messages.

114
00:15:18,114 --> 00:15:21,079
The client process starts the communication.

115
00:15:21,529 --> 00:15:24,324
The server process waits to be contacted.

116
00:15:24,774 --> 00:15:30,662
A process sends and receives messages through its socket, the door to the transport layer.

117
00:15:31,112 --> 00:15:38,899
The host has a unique IP address: 129.97.56.100.

118
00:15:39,349 --> 00:15:46,111
The IP address is not enough. Many processes run on one host, for example a mail server.

119
00:15:46,561 --> 00:15:55,180
So the identifier is the IP address plus the port number. Web server: port 80. Mail server: port 25.

120
00:15:55,630 --> 00:16:05,614
An app needs four things from transport. First, data integrity: file transfer and the web need 100 percent reliable transfer.

121
00:16:06,064 --> 00:16:09,008
Audio can lose some data and still work.

122
00:16:09,458 --> 00:16:16,135
Second, throughput. Multimedia needs a minimum throughput. Elastic apps use whatever they get.

123
00:16:16,585 --> 00:16:21,876
Third, timing. Internet telephony and interactive games need a low delay.

124
00:16:22,326 --> 00:16:25,974
Fourth, security: encryption and data integrity.

125
00:16:26,424 --> 00:16:35,085
The Internet gives two services. TCP: connection-oriented and reliable. UDP: unreliable datagrams.

126
00:16:35,535 --> 00:16:40,549
File transfer and the web need reliable data, so they use TCP.

127
00:16:40,999 --> 00:16:47,057
Internet telephony needs a low delay and can lose some data, so it uses UDP.

128
00:16:47,507 --> 00:16:56,553
A web page is a base HTML file plus referenced objects. 1 base file plus 3 images is 4 objects.

129
00:16:57,003 --> 00:17:05,195
Each object has a URL. First comes the host name, www.someschool.edu.

130
00:17:05,645 --> 00:17:10,146
Then comes the path name, /someDept/pic.gif.

131
00:17:10,596 --> 00:17:18,361
HTTP is the protocol of the web. The browser is the client. The web server sends the objects.

132
00:17:18,811 --> 00:17:26,662
HTTP uses TCP. The client opens a TCP connection to server port 80.

133
00:17:27,112 --> 00:17:33,085
HTTP is stateless: the server keeps no information about past requests.

134
00:17:33,535 --> 00:17:41,194
The browser sends one request per object. Text and 3 images need 4 requests and 4 responses.

135
00:17:41,644 --> 00:17:48,705
Non-persistent HTTP: open a TCP connection, send at most one object, close it.

136
00:17:49,155 --> 00:17:54,105
4 objects from one server need 4 connections, one after the other.

137
00:17:54,555 --> 00:18:01,125
Open takes 3 messages, close takes 2. 4 times 5 is 20 control messages.

138
00:18:01,575 --> 00:18:08,189
Persistent HTTP: one TCP connection carries many objects, then it closes.

139
00:18:08,639 --> 00:18:14,271
That is only 3 plus 2, so 5 control messages, for all 4 objects.

140
00:18:14,721 --> 00:18:24,385
Slide example: a base file and 10 jpeg images is 11 objects. Non-persistent: 11 connections. Persistent: 1.

141
00:18:24,835 --> 00:18:31,341
A connection joins the client and one server. Objects on another server use another connection.

142
00:18:31,791 --> 00:18:37,893
RTT, the round trip time: a small packet goes from client to server and back.

143
00:18:38,343 --> 00:18:44,423
Response time: from the start of the TCP connection until the whole object is in.

144
00:18:44,873 --> 00:18:48,350
One RTT opens the TCP connection.

145
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:54,347
One more RTT sends the request and brings back the first bits of the file.

146
00:18:54,797 --> 00:18:59,959
Then the file transmission time: the file size over the rate, L over R.

147
00:19:00,409 --> 00:19:06,767
So the non-persistent response time is 2 RTT plus the file transmission time.

148
00:19:07,217 --> 00:19:15,942
Our own example: RTT 40 ms, a file of 1 megabit, a rate of 100 Mbps.

149
00:19:16,392 --> 00:19:24,136
L over R: 10 to the 6, over 10 to the 8, is 0.01 seconds, so 10 ms.

150
00:19:24,586 --> 00:19:29,471
Response time: 2 times 40, plus 10, is 90 ms.

151
00:19:29,921 --> 00:19:38,540
A page: a base file and N equals 3 objects, on one server. In the drawing, FTT is half an RTT.

152
00:19:38,990 --> 00:19:45,795
Non-persistent: the base file needs 2 RTT plus its file transmission time, FTT.

153
00:19:46,245 --> 00:19:58,875
Each object repeats this. 4 times, 2 RTT plus FTT, is 8 RTT plus 4 FTT: 8 plus 2, so 10 RTT in the drawing.

154
00:19:59,325 --> 00:20:04,018
Persistent: the base file still needs 2 RTT plus FTT.

155
00:20:04,468 --> 00:20:11,679
The connection stays open. The 3 requests go out together: one more RTT, then the 3 files.

156
00:20:12,129 --> 00:20:20,193
Total: 3 RTT plus 4 FTT. In the drawing, 3 plus 2 is 5 RTT, half of 10.

157
00:20:20,643 --> 00:20:26,253
Parallel connections share the link. With N connections, each one gets R over N.

158
00:20:26,703 --> 00:20:33,615
HTTP has request and response messages. Both are ASCII text that people can read.

159
00:20:34,065 --> 00:20:38,951
The request line: the method, the URL, and the HTTP version.

160
00:20:39,401 --> 00:20:43,433
Header lines follow. Host gives the host name of the server.

161
00:20:43,883 --> 00:20:47,467
Connection: keep-alive asks for a persistent connection.

162
00:20:47,917 --> 00:20:52,077
A carriage return and a line feed alone end the header lines.

163
00:20:52,527 --> 00:20:58,649
GET asks for an object. Small user data goes in the URL, after a question mark.

164
00:20:59,099 --> 00:21:03,345
POST sends user input, such as a form, in the entity body.

165
00:21:03,795 --> 00:21:06,056
HEAD asks only for the header lines.

166
00:21:06,506 --> 00:21:10,474
PUT uploads a file and replaces the file at that URL.

167
00:21:10,924 --> 00:21:16,300
The response starts with the status line: the version, the status code, and the phrase.

168
00:21:16,750 --> 00:21:19,161
Date: when the server sent the response.

169
00:21:19,611 --> 00:21:23,109
Last-Modified: when the object last changed.

170
00:21:23,559 --> 00:21:30,727
Content-Length: the size of the data after the empty line, 2651 bytes.

171
00:21:31,177 --> 00:21:36,340
200, OK: the request succeeded. The object is in this message.

172
00:21:36,790 --> 00:21:42,294
301, Moved Permanently: the new location is in the Location line.

173
00:21:42,744 --> 00:21:47,587
400, Bad Request: the server did not understand the request.

174
00:21:48,037 --> 00:21:52,453
404, Not Found: the document is not on this server.

175
00:21:52,903 --> 00:21:58,172
505: the server does not support this HTTP version.

176
00:21:58,622 --> 00:22:05,875
People use names, such as ece.uwaterloo.ca. Machines use IP addresses.

177
00:22:06,325 --> 00:22:16,117
DNS maps a name to an IP address. It is an application layer protocol. Queries go to UDP port 53.

178
00:22:16,567 --> 00:22:25,805
Host aliasing: www.uwaterloo.ca is an alias of the canonical name uwaterloo.ca.

179
00:22:26,255 --> 00:22:32,079
Mail server aliasing: the mail of a domain goes to a mail server with another name.

180
00:22:32,529 --> 00:22:37,393
Load distribution: one name maps to many replicated web servers.

181
00:22:37,843 --> 00:22:42,728
Why not one central DNS server? It is a single point of failure.

182
00:22:43,178 --> 00:22:50,175
Traffic volume: the DNS servers of Comcast alone get 600 billion queries a day.

183
00:22:50,625 --> 00:22:59,265
That is 600 billion over 86400 seconds: about 6.94 million queries per second.

184
00:22:59,715 --> 00:23:03,577
It is also far from most hosts, and hard to maintain.

185
00:23:04,027 --> 00:23:10,384
So a central DNS does not scale. DNS is distributed and hierarchical.

186
00:23:10,834 --> 00:23:17,917
At the top: 13 logical root servers, A to M. Each one is a cluster of physical servers.

187
00:23:18,367 --> 00:23:26,687
Then the top-level domain servers: .com, .org, .edu, and country domains such as .ca.

188
00:23:27,137 --> 00:23:33,451
Authoritative servers belong to organizations. They give the final answer for their own hosts.

189
00:23:33,901 --> 00:23:40,557
A host sends its query to its local DNS server. It is not strictly in the hierarchy.

190
00:23:41,007 --> 00:23:49,903
Example: www.amazon.com. First, a root server gives the address of a .com server.

191
00:23:50,353 --> 00:23:55,687
Next, the .com server gives the address of the amazon.com server.

192
00:23:56,137 --> 00:24:00,339
Last, the amazon.com server gives the IP address.

193
00:24:00,789 --> 00:24:13,760
A host at ece.uwaterloo.ca wants the IP address of gaia.cs.umass.edu. It sends a recursive query to its local server.

194
00:24:14,210 --> 00:24:23,469
Iterated query: each server replies with the next server to ask. The root says: ask the .edu TLD server.

195
00:24:23,919 --> 00:24:29,188
The local server then asks the TLD server, and then the authoritative server.

196
00:24:29,638 --> 00:24:36,358
It returns the IP address to the host: 8 messages, 4 queries and 4 replies.

197
00:24:36,808 --> 00:24:44,381
Recursive query: each contacted server asks the next one itself. The burden is on the contacted server.

198
00:24:44,831 --> 00:24:52,938
Also 8 messages, but the root handles 4, not 2. Too much load at the top, so it is not done in practice.

199
00:24:53,388 --> 00:25:00,321
Name servers cache each mapping that they learn. The top line gives our own example RTTs.

200
00:25:00,771 --> 00:25:08,665
Empty cache: ask all three. 2 plus 40 plus 30 plus 50 is 122 ms.

201
00:25:09,115 --> 00:25:16,112
TLD servers cached: skip the root. 2 plus 30 plus 50 is 82 ms.

202
00:25:16,562 --> 00:25:25,479
Each cache entry has a time to live, the TTL: 3600 seconds in the example. Then it is removed.

203
00:25:25,929 --> 00:25:38,772
The IP address changes at 1200 seconds. The cache stays stale for 3600 minus 1200, so 2400 seconds.

204
00:25:39,222 --> 00:25:47,371
A cached entry can be out of date until all TTLs expire. So DNS is a best-effort translation.

205
00:25:47,821 --> 00:25:55,864
Click a link. The address is not cached, so DNS visits n servers: RTT 1 to RTT n.

206
00:25:56,314 --> 00:26:04,933
Then HTTP: one RTT 0 opens TCP, and one RTT 0 brings the small object.

207
00:26:05,383 --> 00:26:11,612
Total: RTT 1, and so on up to RTT n, plus 2 RTT 0.

208
00:26:12,062 --> 00:26:18,505
Our own example: 10, 30 and 20 ms. DNS takes 60 ms.

209
00:26:18,955 --> 00:26:28,725
RTT 0 is 50 ms. 2 times 50 is 100 ms. The total is 160 ms.

210
00:26:29,175 --> 00:26:36,578
DNS comes first: the client needs the IP address before it opens the TCP connection.

211
00:26:37,028 --> 00:26:42,233
DNS stores resource records: name, TTL, type and value.

212
00:26:42,683 --> 00:26:47,526
Type A: the name is a host name, and the value is its IP address.

213
00:26:47,976 --> 00:26:54,760
Type NS: the name is a domain, and the value is the host name of its authoritative server.

214
00:26:55,210 --> 00:27:01,034
Type CNAME: the name is an alias, and the value is the canonical, real name.

215
00:27:01,484 --> 00:27:05,303
Type MX: the value is the name of the mail server.

216
00:27:05,753 --> 00:27:12,153
Queries and replies share one format. The header has 6 fields of 2 bytes: 12 bytes.

217
00:27:12,603 --> 00:27:20,325
A new company: the registrar puts an NS record and an A record into the .com TLD server.

218
00:27:20,775 --> 00:27:27,986
This section is the first half of Chapter 3, the transport layer, slides 1 to 50. Here is its map.

219
00:27:28,436 --> 00:27:33,236
TCP, from slide 51, comes in video 26 b.

220
00:27:33,686 --> 00:27:40,235
The transport layer gives logical communication between application processes on different hosts.

221
00:27:40,685 --> 00:27:46,445
Transport protocols run only in the end systems: here, host A and host B.

222
00:27:46,895 --> 00:27:52,805
The router has only the network, link and physical layers. It has no transport layer.

223
00:27:53,255 --> 00:27:58,439
The sender gets a message from the application. It breaks the message into segments.

224
00:27:58,889 --> 00:28:06,419
It puts a transport header, H t, on each segment. Then it passes the segments to the network layer, I P.

225
00:28:06,869 --> 00:28:12,992
The receiver puts the segments back into the message, and passes it up to the process through a socket.

226
00:28:13,442 --> 00:28:21,015
The Internet has two transport protocols, TCP and UDP. The table compares their services.

227
00:28:21,465 --> 00:28:30,831
TCP gives reliable, in-order delivery. UDP is unreliable: segments can be lost or arrive out of order.

228
00:28:31,281 --> 00:28:36,657
TCP has congestion control and flow control. UDP has neither.

229
00:28:37,107 --> 00:28:43,016
TCP sets up a connection first, with a handshake. UDP is connectionless.

230
00:28:43,466 --> 00:28:48,010
Neither protocol gives a delay guarantee or a bandwidth guarantee.

231
00:28:48,460 --> 00:28:59,596
TCP carries H T T P and F T P. UDP carries streaming multimedia, D N S, S N M P, and H T T P 3.

232
00:29:00,046 --> 00:29:07,278
Files and web pages must not lose data: TCP. Real-time voice and video: UDP.

233
00:29:07,728 --> 00:29:15,429
Multiplexing: the sender takes data from many sockets. It adds a header with a source port and a destination port.

234
00:29:15,879 --> 00:29:23,346
A port number has 16 bits, so it is a number from 0 to 65535.

235
00:29:23,796 --> 00:29:32,479
The receiver uses the header to pick the socket. In UDP, two clients send to port 6428.

236
00:29:32,929 --> 00:29:40,545
UDP looks only at the destination I P address and port. So both segments go to the same socket.

237
00:29:40,995 --> 00:29:50,360
TCP identifies a socket by the 4-tuple: the source I P address and port, and the destination I P address and port.

238
00:29:50,810 --> 00:30:00,901
Three segments go to server B, port 80. Each has its own 4-tuple, so each goes to its own socket: P 4, P 5, and P 6.

239
00:30:01,351 --> 00:30:11,932
A and C both use source port 9157. Their source I P addresses differ, so the two segments go to two different sockets.

240
00:30:12,382 --> 00:30:18,505
The UDP header has four fields: source port, destination port, length, and checksum.

241
00:30:18,955 --> 00:30:25,397
Each field has 16 bits. 4 times 16 is 64 bits, which is 8 bytes.

242
00:30:25,847 --> 00:30:30,562
The length field gives the length of the whole segment in bytes, header included.

243
00:30:31,012 --> 00:30:39,652
Example: 120 bytes of data give a length of 8 plus 120, which is 128 bytes.

244
00:30:40,102 --> 00:30:51,046
The length field has 16 bits, so its largest value is 2 to the 16, minus 1. That is 65535 bytes.

245
00:30:51,496 --> 00:31:02,163
So one segment carries at most 65535 minus 8, which is 65527 data bytes.

246
00:31:02,613 --> 00:31:05,962
The checksum field detects flipped bits in the segment.

247
00:31:06,412 --> 00:31:13,196
UDP has no setup delay, no connection state, a small header, and no congestion control.

248
00:31:13,646 --> 00:31:19,129
The sender treats the segment as a sequence of 16 bit words. Here are two words.

249
00:31:19,579 --> 00:31:25,424
First, add the words. The sum has 17 bits: a carry comes out of the top bit.

250
00:31:25,874 --> 00:31:31,207
Add the carry back to the lowest bit. This wraparound gives the one's complement sum.

251
00:31:31,657 --> 00:31:37,887
Then invert every bit. The result is the checksum, and the sender puts it in the checksum field.

252
00:31:38,337 --> 00:31:45,142
The receiver adds the words and the checksum, with the same wraparound. With no error, every bit is 1.

253
00:31:45,592 --> 00:31:52,547
If any bit is 0, the receiver detects an error. All ones means only that no error was detected.

254
00:31:52,997 --> 00:32:02,725
An unreliable channel can corrupt bits, deliver packets out of order, lose them, or duplicate them. The mnemonic is B O L D.

255
00:32:03,175 --> 00:32:08,529
Reliable transfer needs feedback from the receiver: the acknowledgement, or ACK.

256
00:32:08,979 --> 00:32:13,054
Sequence numbers solve out-of-order and duplicate packets.

257
00:32:13,504 --> 00:32:19,755
Timers solve lost packets. If no ACK comes before the timeout, the sender retransmits.

258
00:32:20,205 --> 00:32:26,946
Error detection codes, such as the checksum, detect bit errors. The receiver discards that packet.

259
00:32:27,396 --> 00:32:34,457
Protocols that retransmit like this are called A R Q protocols: automatic repeat request.

260
00:32:34,907 --> 00:32:42,011
Stop-and-wait: send one packet, start a timer, and wait for the ACK. Here the sender sends packet 0.

261
00:32:42,461 --> 00:32:49,181
Packet 0 arrives, and the receiver sends ack 0. The sender gets it, and sends packet 1.

262
00:32:49,631 --> 00:32:52,853
Packet 1 is lost. The receiver sends nothing.

263
00:32:53,303 --> 00:32:57,847
The timer expires. The sender resends one packet: packet 1.

264
00:32:58,297 --> 00:33:03,310
Packet 1 arrives, and the receiver sends ack 1. But ack 1 is lost.

265
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:07,941
The timer expires again, and the sender resends packet 1.

266
00:33:08,391 --> 00:33:15,879
The receiver sees sequence number 1 again: a duplicate. It discards the packet, but it sends ack 1 again.

267
00:33:16,329 --> 00:33:26,228
The sequence numbers go 0, 1, 0, 1. One bit is enough, so stop-and-wait is also called the alternating bit protocol.

268
00:33:26,678 --> 00:33:36,918
A timeout that comes too early also makes duplicates. A duplicate packet at the receiver, or a duplicate ACK at the sender, is discarded.

269
00:33:37,368 --> 00:33:43,619
U sender, the sender utilization, is the fraction of time that the sender is busy sending.

270
00:33:44,069 --> 00:33:50,533
At t equals 0, the sender starts to send the packet. All L bits take L over R.

271
00:33:50,983 --> 00:33:56,401
The bits travel to the receiver. When the last bit arrives, the receiver sends the ACK.

272
00:33:56,851 --> 00:34:05,833
The ACK arrives one R T T later, at t equals R T T plus L over R. Only then can the sender send again.

273
00:34:06,283 --> 00:34:11,957
So U sender is L over R, divided by R T T plus L over R.

274
00:34:12,407 --> 00:34:24,802
Slide example: L over R is 8000 bits over 10 to the 9 bits per second. That is 8 microseconds, or 0 point 0 0 8 milliseconds.

275
00:34:25,252 --> 00:34:30,863
The R T T is 2 times d prop: 2 times 15 is 30 milliseconds.

276
00:34:31,313 --> 00:34:40,465
U sender is 0 point 0 0 8 over 30 point 0 0 8: about 0 point 0 2 7 percent.

277
00:34:40,915 --> 00:34:48,083
Drawn to scale, the busy part of one cycle is a thin line. The sender waits almost all the time.

278
00:34:48,533 --> 00:34:53,653
Throughput is U times R: about 270 kilobits per second.

279
00:34:54,103 --> 00:35:01,015
Pipelining: the sender has several packets in flight that are not yet ACKed. We use our own example.

280
00:35:01,465 --> 00:35:11,854
L over R is 1000 bits over 500 thousand bits per second: 0 point 0 0 2 seconds, or 2 milliseconds.

281
00:35:12,304 --> 00:35:19,109
One cycle is R T T plus L over R: 14 plus 2 is 16 milliseconds.

282
00:35:19,559 --> 00:35:27,538
The bar shows one cycle, from 0 to 16 milliseconds. Each slot is one packet time: 2 milliseconds.

283
00:35:27,988 --> 00:35:36,521
Stop-and-wait has a window of 1: one packet per cycle. U is 2 over 16, which is 0 point 1 2 5.

284
00:35:36,971 --> 00:35:49,985
With a window W of 4, the sender sends 4 packets per cycle. U is W times L over R, over the cycle: 4 times 2 over 16 is 0 point 5.

285
00:35:50,435 --> 00:35:59,629
Throughput is U times R: 0 point 5 times 500 kilobits per second is 250 kilobits per second.

286
00:36:00,079 --> 00:36:05,498
A window of 8 fills the whole cycle. U is 1, or 100 percent.

287
00:36:05,948 --> 00:36:15,164
This is the ideal window size: R T T plus L over R, divided by L over R. 16 over 2 is 8.

288
00:36:15,614 --> 00:36:24,211
A window of 10 gives 20 over 16, which is 1 point 2 5. But U is the minimum with 1, so U is 1.

289
00:36:24,661 --> 00:36:31,083
Pipelining needs a larger range of sequence numbers, and buffers at the sender and the receiver.

290
00:36:31,533 --> 00:36:37,271
Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat both allow up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline.

291
00:36:37,721 --> 00:36:44,057
Slide example: the sender sends packets 0 to 7, and packets 4 and 6 are lost.

292
00:36:44,507 --> 00:36:49,499
The Go-Back-N receiver ACKs each in-order packet: ack 0 to ack 3.

293
00:36:49,949 --> 00:36:55,923
Packets 5 and 7 are out of order. Go-Back-N discards them, and sends ack 3 again.

294
00:36:56,373 --> 00:37:02,602
These ACKs are cumulative: ack 3 means that all packets up to and including 3 arrived.

295
00:37:03,052 --> 00:37:09,943
The Selective Repeat receiver ACKs each correct packet: ack 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, and 7.

296
00:37:10,393 --> 00:37:14,531
It buffers packets 5 and 7, for delivery in order later.

297
00:37:14,981 --> 00:37:21,829
Go-Back-N has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. A timeout resends all unACKed packets.

298
00:37:22,279 --> 00:37:28,402
Selective Repeat has one timer per unACKed packet. A timeout resends only that packet.

299
00:37:28,852 --> 00:37:36,468
The Go-Back-N sender keeps a window of up to N consecutive sequence numbers. In our example, N is 4.

300
00:37:36,918 --> 00:37:42,166
Green numbers are ACKed. send base points to the oldest unACKed number: 2.

301
00:37:42,616 --> 00:37:46,840
Orange numbers 2, 3, and 4 are sent, but not yet ACKed.

302
00:37:47,290 --> 00:37:52,197
Blue 5 is in the window, but not yet sent. next seq num points to it.

303
00:37:52,647 --> 00:38:02,076
ACK 3 arrives. It is cumulative, so 2 and 3 are both ACKed. The window moves to 4 to 7, and the sender sends 5 and 6.

304
00:38:02,526 --> 00:38:12,681
Then the timer of packet 4, the oldest unACKed packet, expires. The sender resends 4 and all higher sent packets: 4, 5, and 6.

305
00:38:13,131 --> 00:38:23,115
A k bit sequence number field gives the numbers 0 to 2 to the k, minus 1. For k equal to 5, that is 0 to 31.

306
00:38:23,565 --> 00:38:33,655
Selective Repeat, from slide 47. The window size N is 4. The sender sends packets 0 to 3, and packet 2 is lost.

307
00:38:34,105 --> 00:38:41,295
Packets 0 and 1 arrive in order. The receiver delivers them, and sends ack 0 and ack 1.

308
00:38:41,745 --> 00:38:47,633
Each of these ACKs moves the sender window by one, so the sender sends packets 4 and 5.

309
00:38:48,083 --> 00:38:54,675
Packets 3, 4, and 5 arrive after the gap. The receiver buffers them, and ACKs each one.

310
00:38:55,125 --> 00:39:02,677
The sender marks 3, 4, and 5 as ACKed. But send base stays at 2, because packet 2 is not ACKed.

311
00:39:03,127 --> 00:39:10,700
Each packet has its own timer. The timer of packet 2 expires, and the sender resends only packet 2.

312
00:39:11,150 --> 00:39:18,339
Packet 2 fills the gap. The receiver delivers 2, 3, 4, and 5 in order, and sends ack 2.

313
00:39:18,789 --> 00:39:26,683
When ack 2 arrives, send base jumps from 2 to 6, the next unACKed number. The window is now 6 to 9.

314
00:39:27,133 --> 00:39:32,871
So a window moves only when its base is ACKed at the sender, or received at the receiver.

315
00:39:33,321 --> 00:39:42,644
Sequence numbers repeat. With 2 bits, they are 0, 1, 2, 3, then 0 again. The slide uses a window size of 3.

316
00:39:43,094 --> 00:39:51,265
The sender sends packets 0, 1, and 2, and all three arrive. The receiver window moves on to 3, 0, 1.

317
00:39:51,715 --> 00:39:57,475
Case a: the three ACKs arrive, so the sender window also moves to 3, 0, 1.

318
00:39:57,925 --> 00:40:06,266
The sender sends a new packet 3, which is lost, and a new packet 0. The receiver accepts the new 0 as new data.

319
00:40:06,716 --> 00:40:12,305
Case b: all three ACKs are lost. The sender window stays at 0, 1, 2.

320
00:40:12,755 --> 00:40:18,345
The timer of packet 0 expires, and the sender resends the old packet 0.

321
00:40:18,795 --> 00:40:26,773
The receiver window is the same as in case a. The old 0 falls in it, so the receiver accepts it as new data.

322
00:40:27,223 --> 00:40:34,434
So the data of the first packet 0 is delivered twice. The receiver cannot tell case a from case b.

323
00:40:34,884 --> 00:40:39,001
The fix on the slide: make the window size 2, half of 4.

324
00:40:39,451 --> 00:40:46,257
Again packets 0 and 1 arrive, and both ACKs are lost. The receiver window moves to 2, 3.

325
00:40:46,707 --> 00:40:57,629
The sender resends the old packet 0. Now 0 is outside the receiver window, so it is a duplicate. The receiver ACKs it again and discards it.

326
00:40:58,079 --> 00:41:07,743
The rule: the window size must be at most half of the sequence number space. In the past midterm, W s is at most K over 2.

327
00:41:08,193 --> 00:41:17,537
With a k bit field, the space has 2 to the k numbers. So W is at most 2 to the k, over 2: 2 to the k minus 1.

328
00:41:17,987 --> 00:41:23,662
An 8 bit field gives 2 to the 8, which is 256 sequence numbers.

329
00:41:24,112 --> 00:41:31,216
2048 is 2 to the 11, so 2048 sequence numbers need 11 bits.

330
00:41:31,666 --> 00:41:42,034
4096 sequence numbers allow a Selective Repeat window of at most 4096 over 2, which is 2048.

331
00:41:42,484 --> 00:41:49,673
That is the end of part 1: Chapter 1, Chapter 2, and reliable transfer up to Selective Repeat.

332
00:41:50,123 --> 00:42:00,918
Video 26 b has TCP, then the exam questions, and the formula sheets. TCP congestion control is not on this midterm.
